package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneTerrainRivers is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/terrainRivers.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn to draw and change rivers on a Quorum Studio terrain with the River tool, and how a few rules about real rivers become steps a computer can follow.")

        Title("Rivers")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Rivers tie a landscape together. They start high in the hills, wind
downhill through the valleys, and end in a lake or the sea. In Quorum
Studio, you draw a river as a path: a line of points across the ground.
Quorum Studio then works out the rest. It makes the water's surface run
downhill, cuts a bed into the ground so the banks hold the water, and makes
the ripples flow downstream. In this tutorial, you will draw a river with
the mouse or the keyboard, change it, and learn how a few simple rules about
real rivers become steps a computer can follow.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Draw a river with the River tool, with the mouse or the keyboard")
        ListItem("Explain how a river is stored as a path of points, and what Quorum Studio
works out from it")
        ListItem("Describe the rules that keep a river's water running downhill, and what
happens where the path climbs")
        ListItem("Change a river's points, width, depth and speed, and remove it")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a real river, one near you or one you know from a map or a
story. Where does it
start, and where does it end? Does it ever flow uphill? What do you think
happens to the land when a river has to get through a hill that is in its
way?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/Rivers/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_DESIGN_AND_DEVELOPMENT_2)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_2)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("This tutorial needs a terrain with hills and valleys, and it helps to have
a lake from "):Link("Lakes", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainLakes.html"):Text(" or a sea from
"):Link("The Sea", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainSea.html"):Text(" for the river to flow into.
Rivers share the water's look with the sea and the lakes.")
        RiverIsAPath()
        DrawingARiver()
        WhenThePathClimbs()
        WhereARiverStartsAndEnds()
        ChangingARiver()
        RiversInTheWaterSection()
    end

    private action RiverIsAPath
        Subsection("A river is a path")
        Paragraph("A real river is billions of drops of water, each pushed by gravity. A
computer can't follow every drop, so Quorum Studio keeps only what matters:
the river's "):Term("path"):Text(", a short list of points from its start to its end,
and its width, depth and speed. From those few numbers, it works out
everything else:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("A smooth line."):Text(" Quorum Studio draws a smooth curve through the points,
so the river bends gently instead of turning sharp corners at each one.")
        ListItem():Strong("The water's surface."):Text(" Along the curve, the water's height starts at the
ground's height and is smoothed. Its one firm rule is that it "):Strong("never
rises going downstream"):Text(". Water can't flow uphill.")
        ListItem():Strong("The bed and banks."):Text(" Under the water, the ground is lowered to make a
bed, deepest in the middle and rising to the banks at the sides. This is
called "):Term("carving"):Text(". Because of it, the banks always hold the water, and
the river never floats above the ground.")
        ListItem():Strong("The flow."):Text(" The ripples on a river move along it, downstream, instead
of drifting all one way as they do on a lake. They move faster where the
river is narrow or steep.")
        Paragraph("Keeping only the path is an example of "):Term("abstraction"):Text(": a few points stand
in for the whole river, and the rules fill in the details.")
    end

    private action DrawingARiver
        Subsection("Drawing a river")
        Paragraph("The River tool is the terrain's sixteenth tool. Select the terrain, then
choose the tool.")
        Table("Keys for the River tool", "media/scenes/Terrain/Rivers/Keys.csv")

        Paragraph():Strong("With the mouse:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Choose the River tool: press "):Keys("Shift", "Alt", "7"):Text(" ("):Keys("Shift", "Option", "7"):Text(" on Mac), or
choose "):Strong("River"):Text(" in the Brush section's Tool group.")
        ListItem("Click where the river starts, usually high up in the hills.")
        ListItem("Click again at each bend, working downhill. A faint ghost of the next
stretch follows the mouse, showing where the river would go.")
        ListItem("Press "):Key("Enter"):Text(" ("):Key("Return"):Text(" on Mac) to finish the river.")

        Paragraph():Strong("With the keyboard:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Choose the River tool with "):Keys("Shift", "Alt", "7"):Text(" ("):Keys("Shift", "Option", "7"):Text(" on Mac).")
        ListItem("Move the terrain's cursor with the arrow keys to where the river starts,
and press "):Key("Space"):Text(" to add the first point.")
        ListItem("Move to each bend and press "):Key("Space"):Text(" again. Quorum Studio reports how
long the stretch since the last point is and how much the ground drops,
which a screen reader may announce as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Point 2, 12.5 units from the last; the ground drops 1.2. Press
Enter to finish the river."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Where the ground rises instead, the report says
"):DoubleQuote():Output("the ground rises 0.8, so the river will cut into it there"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
        ListItem("Press "):Key("Enter"):Text(" to finish the river. Quorum Studio reports it, for
example "):DoubleQuote():Output("Drew River 1: 3 points, 45.2 units long, water from height 20.1
down to 15.3."):DoubleQuote()

        Paragraph("If you add a point by mistake, press "):Key("Delete"):Text(" or "):Key("Backspace"):Text(" to take back the last point.")
        Paragraph("To give up on a river while drawing it, press "):Key("Escape"):Text(". The points you
placed so far are taken away, and Quorum Studio reports "):DoubleQuote():Output("Stopped drawing the
river. Nothing was added."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("When you finish the river, Quorum Studio carves its bed into the ground
right away. Finishing the river, with its carving, is one step for Undo.")
        // Image to add: /media/scenes/Terrain/Rivers/DrawingRiver.png — A river being drawn down a hillside, with three placed points and a faint line to the mouse pointer.
    end

    private action WhenThePathClimbs
        Subsection("When the path climbs")
        Paragraph("Sometimes the path goes over a rise: the ground between two points is
higher than the point before it. Real rivers don't flow uphill. Over a long
time, they cut down through the rise, which is how canyons form. Quorum
Studio does the same thing at once. The water's surface stays level through
the rise, and the bed is carved deeper there, so the river cuts through the
hill. Quorum Studio reports where this happens and how deep the cut is, for
example "):DoubleQuote():Output("The river cuts 3.2 units into the ground between points 4 and 5, where the ground rises."):DoubleQuote():Text("
A very deep cut is a hint that the path might go around the hill instead.")
    end

    private action WhereARiverStartsAndEnds
        Subsection("Where a river starts and ends")
        Paragraph("A river can start from a lake, and it can end in a lake or in the sea. If
its last point is in a lake or the sea, the river ends at that water's
level, so the two meet smoothly. For now, each river is one line from its
start to its end: rivers can't split into branches or join each other yet.")
        // Image to add: /media/scenes/Terrain/Rivers/RiverToSea.png — A river winding down a green valley into the sea, with banks carved on both sides.
    end

    private action ChangingARiver
        Subsection("Changing a river")
        Paragraph("To change a river after it is drawn, choose the River tool again, or press
"):Strong("Edit path"):Text(" in the river's part of the Water section. Every river's path
then shows as a light blue line over the ground, with a small square at
each point, and Quorum Studio reports which rivers there are, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("1
river: River 1, 7 points. Its path is shown; click a point to choose it,
then drag it, or press Delete."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("With the River tool chosen:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Choosing a point."):Text(" Click or select one of a river's points. The chosen
point's square is larger than the others. Quorum Studio reports which point it is, for
example "):DoubleQuote():Output("Point 2 of 3 of River 1. Drag it to move it, or press Delete to
remove it."):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():Strong("Moving a point."):Text(" Hold the mouse button on a point and drag it. When
you let go, the river and its bed follow.")
        ListItem():Strong("Adding a point."):Text(" Click on the river's water, between two points, to
add a point there, for a new bend.")
        ListItem():Strong("Letting go."):Text(" Click empty ground to let go of the chosen river. Quorum
Studio reports "):DoubleQuote():Output("No river chosen. Click empty ground again to start a new
river."):DoubleQuote():Text(" A new river only starts when no river is chosen, so a stray
click while editing doesn't start one.")
        ListItem():Strong("Removing a point."):Text(" Choose a point and press "):Key("Delete"):Text(" or "):Key("Backspace"):Text(". A river left with fewer than two points is
removed.")
        Paragraph("With the keyboard, move the terrain cursor onto a river's point and press
"):Key("Space"):Text(" to choose it, then "):Key("Delete"):Text(" to remove it. Moving a point needs the
mouse for now.")
        Paragraph("Each change carves the bed again, as one step for Undo. Quorum Studio keeps
the ground's heights from before the river was carved, so when you move or
remove a river, it puts the old ground back. If you shaped the ground under
the river after carving it, Quorum Studio leaves that ground as it is.
Removing a river is reported as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Removed River 1. Its bed was filled back in where
the ground wasn't changed since."):DoubleQuote()
    end

    private action RiversInTheWaterSection
        Subsection("Rivers in the Water section")
        Paragraph("The Water section's "):Strong("Rivers"):Text(" part lists each river as a part of its own,
which opens with its chevron. Its title sums the river up, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("River
1, 241.6 long, water 30.1 down to 10"):DoubleQuote():Text(". Opened, it has:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Name"):Text(", such as "):Quoted("Mill river"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Strong("Width"):Text(", how wide the water is;")
        ListItem():Strong("Depth"):Text(", how deep the bed is cut below the water;")
        ListItem():Strong("Speed"):Text(", how fast the water flows;")
        ListItem():Strong("Edit path"):Text(", which chooses the River tool and the river's first point,
so you can go straight to changing its points in the scene;")
        ListItem():Strong("Remove river"):Text(", which takes the river away.")
        Paragraph("The "):Strong("New rivers"):Text(" part sets the width and depth the River tool draws new
rivers with.")
        Paragraph("Saving the scene saves the rivers in the terrain's "):Code("water.json"):Text(" file,
beside the lakes. A game that loads the scene gets its rivers with no extra
code, and the water questions from the Lakes tutorial, such as
"):Code("GetWaterDepthAt"):Text(", count river water too.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("The goal of this activity is to turn what you know about real rivers into
steps a computer could follow, and then to test those steps against what
Quorum Studio does. Work with a partner.")
        Paragraph():Strong("Part 1: Rules on paper."):Text(" Make a map of a small landscape with height
numbers on it: the tops of the hills, the bottoms of the valleys, and a lake
or the sea at one edge. Decide together where a river should start and end,
and choose four to six points on its path, each with its height. Then write
rules a computer could use to make the river from those points alone. What
does it need to know at each point? How does it decide the water's height
between points? What should it do where the path goes over higher ground?
Write your rules as a numbered list.")
        Paragraph():Strong("Part 2: Predict, then test."):Text(" Use your rules to predict, for each stretch
between points, the water's height at each end, and whether the river will
cut into the ground there and by how much. Write your predictions in a
table. Then shape a terrain like your map in Quorum Studio, and draw the
river through the same points. Record what Quorum Studio reports for each
stretch and for the finished river, and add it to your table beside your
predictions.")
        Paragraph():Strong("Part 3: Change one thing."):Text(" Find the stretch with the deepest cut. Move
one point so the path goes around the rise instead of through it, and
record the new report.")
        Paragraph("Discuss:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("Where did your predictions and Quorum Studio's results differ? Which rule
was different, and which is closer to a real river?")
        ListItem("Which details of a real river did your rules leave out? Which would
matter to a player, and which only to someone studying rivers?")
        ListItem("When you moved the point, what changed about the cut? Would a real river
go through the rise or around it?")
        ListItem("What would you want Quorum Studio to report while drawing a river that it
doesn't report today?")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A river in Quorum Studio is a short list of points and a few rules: water
runs downhill, the surface never rises downstream, and where the land is
in the way, the river cuts through it. That is how computer scientists
often model the natural world: keep the few details that matter, and let
rules fill in the rest. In the last tutorial of this series,
"):Link("Changing the Palette", "/tutorials/Scenes/palette.html"):Text(", you will add your
own 2D images to the scene editor.")
    end
end
